   "```python\ndef fibonacci(n, memo=None):\n    \"\"\"\n    Compute the n-th Fibonacci number using memoization.\n\n    Definition:\n        F(0) = 0\n        F(1) = 1\n        F(n) = F(n-1) + F(n-2) for n >= 2\n\n    Parameters:\n        n (int): The index of the Fibonacci number (non-negative).\n        memo (dict, optional): Dictionary for memoization. Used internally.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The n-th Fibonacci number.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a non-negative integer\")\n\n    if memo is None:\n        memo = {}\n\n    if n in memo:\n        return memo[n]\n\n    if n == 0:\n        result = 0\n    elif n == 1:\n        result = 1\n    else:\n        result = fibonacci(n - 1, memo) + fibonacci(n - 2, memo)\n\n    memo[n] = result\n    return result\n\n\n# Example usage:\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    for i in range(10",
   "To find the average speed for the whole trip, we must use the fundamental formula for average speed:\n\n$$ \\text{Average Speed} = \\frac{\\text{Total Distance}}{\\text{Total Time}} $$\n\nIt is important to note that average speed is **not** the simple average of the two speeds calculated for each leg of the trip. We must sum the distances and the times first.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the Total Distance\nThe train travels two segments:\n1.  First segment: $120 \\text{ km}$\n2.  Second segment: $80 \\text{ km}$\n\n$$ \\text{Total Distance} = 120 \\text{ km} + 80 \\text{ km} = 200 \\text{ km} $$\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the Total Time\nThe train travels for two time intervals:\n1.  First segment: $1.5 \\text{ hours}$\n2.  Second segment: $45 \\text{ minutes}$\n\nTo add these times together, they must be in the same unit. Let's convert the second segment into hours.\nSince there are 60 minutes in an hour:\n$$",
